      "completion_tokens": 611,
      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and the planet’s climate. Here’s a concise breakdown of what makes water special:\n\n| Aspect | What it means |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Chemical identity** | Two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom (H₂O). |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° angle) giving it a polar, “dipole” character. |\n| **Polarity** | The oxygen atom carries a partial negative charge; the hydrogens carry partial positive charges. This polarity allows water to dissolve many ionic and polar substances. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds (two as donor, two as acceptor). These bonds give water its high surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion. |\n| **Physical states** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). The transitions are governed by temperature and pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • High specific heat (absorbs a lot of heat before warming up). <br>• High heat of vaporization (requires a lot of energy to evaporate). <br>• High surface tension. <br>• Universal solvent. <br>• Density anomaly (ice floats). |\n| **Biological role** | • Solvent for cellular processes. <br>• Medium for biochemical reactions. <br>• Regulates temperature via sweating, respiration, and circulation. |\n| **Environmental role** | • Drives the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Influences weather, climate, and ecosystems. |\n| **Human use** | • Drinking, agriculture, industry, sanitation, energy (steam turbines). |\n\n### Why water is so important\n\n1. **Chemical versatility** – Its polarity and ability to form hydrogen bonds make it an excellent solvent for salts, sugars, proteins, and many other molecules.\n2. **Thermal stability** – The high specific heat and heat of vaporization help moderate Earth’s temperature and keep organisms from overheating.\n3. **Physical behavior** – The density anomaly (ice being less dense than liquid water) preserves aquatic life in winter by insulating the water below.\n4. **Biological necessity** – Every known life form relies on water for metabolism, transport, and structural integrity.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that, through its unique chemical and physical properties, sustains life, shapes the planet’s climate, and underpins countless natural and human-made processes."
